(function(){window.PhysicaRankPyqNeet_chemistry_chemicalkinetics=[{id:"neet-2026r-q056",pn:56,y:"Re-NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun",tp:"Chemical Kinetics",st:"Arrhenius equation and plot",q:`For an elementary chemical reaction, the Arrhenius plot is given below.
If the energy of activation is 6.64 kJ mol\u207B\xB9 and R = 8.3 J K\u207B\xB9 mol\u207B\xB9, the temperature at which the rate constant becomes e\xB2 min\u207B\xB9, is`,o:["150 K","200 K","250 K","125 K"],c:1,s:`ln k = ln A \u2013 Ea/RT.
The graph shows ln k = 6 at the intercept (1/T = 0), so ln A = 6.
For k = e\xB2, ln k = 2.
2 = 6 \u2013 (6.64\xD710\xB3)/(8.3 \xD7 T).
(6.64\xD710\xB3)/(8.3 \xD7 T) = 4.
T = (6.64\xD710\xB3)/(8.3 \xD7 4) = 200 K.`,fig:{w:300,h:204,alt:"Arrhenius plot: ln k on the vertical axis (ticks 2, 4, 6, 8) against 1/T; a straight line falls from ln k = 6 on the vertical axis to about ln k = 1 at the right.",d:[["l",60,170,60,8],["l",60,170,280,170],["l",56,152,60,152],["l",56,134,60,134],["t",52,138,"2","e"],["l",56,116,60,116],["l",56,98,60,98],["t",52,102,"4","e"],["l",56,80,60,80],["l",56,62,60,62],["t",52,66,"6","e"],["l",56,44,60,44],["l",56,26,60,26],["t",52,30,"8","e"],["l",60,62,250,150.2,"B"],["l",22,125,22,71,"a"],["t",30,54.8,"lnk","s"],["l",120,192,148,192,"a"],["t",154,196,"1/T"]]}},{id:"neet-2026-q056",pn:56,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 3 May",tp:"Chemical Kinetics",st:"Units of rate constant",q:`Match List I with List II.
List-I (Order of reaction):
A. Zero order
B. First order
C. Second order
D. Third order
List-II (Unit of rate constant):
I. mol\u207B\xB9 L s\u207B\xB9
II. mol\u207B\xB2 L\xB2 s\u207B\xB9
III. s\u207B\xB9
IV. mol L\u207B\xB9 s\u207B\xB9
Choose the correct answer from the options given below:`,o:["A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I","A-I, B-II, C-III, D-IV","A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-I","A-IV, B-II, C-I, D-III"],c:2,s:`The unit of the rate constant of an nth order reaction is (mol/L)^(1\u2013n) s\u207B\xB9.
Zero order, n = 0: unit is mol L\u207B\xB9 s\u207B\xB9.
First order, n = 1: unit is s\u207B\xB9.
Second order, n = 2: unit is mol\u207B\xB9 L s\u207B\xB9.
Third order, n = 3: unit is mol\u207B\xB2 L\xB2 s\u207B\xB9.
(The publisher wrote n = 2 for the third-order line, but the unit given is the n = 3 one.)
Note: Printed option 3 reads D-I, but third order matches II (mol\u207B\xB2 L\xB2 s\u207B\xB9); printed answer [3] kept (A-IV, B-III, C-I are right, D looks like a typo)`},{id:"neet-2026r-q067",pn:67,y:"Re-NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun",tp:"Chemical Kinetics",st:"Zero order reaction",q:"2A \u2192 B (rate constant k) is a zero-order reaction, where k = 1.0 mol L\u207B\xB9 min\u207B\xB9. If the initial concentration of A is 2 M, then the time taken to complete 75% of the reaction will be",o:["0.75 min","1.0 min","2.0 min","1.5 min"],c:0,s:`For the zero order reaction, \u2212(1/2)(dA/dt) = k.
t = (A\u2080 \u2212 A\u209C)/2k.
75% complete leaves A\u209C = 0.5 M.
t = (2 \u2212 0.5)/2 = 0.75 min.`},{id:"neet-2026-q083",pn:83,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 3 May",tp:"Chemical Kinetics",st:"Zero order reaction graph",q:"For a certain reaction R \u2192 Product, the plot of concentration [R] vs time has a negative slope as shown. The order of reaction is :",o:["0","1","2","2.5"],c:0,s:`For a zero order reaction, [R] = [R\u2080] \u2013 kt.
So a straight line with a negative slope (k = \u2013slope) shows a zero order reaction.`,fig:{w:260,h:170,alt:"Graph of [R] concentration against time: a straight line falling from [R0] on the vertical axis, labelled k = \u2013slope.",d:[["l",90,150,90,12,"a"],["l",90,150,255,150,"a"],["t",98,22,"[R\u2080]",""],["l",90,30,215,110,""],["t",165,66,"k = \u2013slope",""],["t",84,76,"[R]","e"],["t",84,90,"concentration","es"],["l",140,162,170,162,"a"],["t",176,166,"time",""]]},sfig:{w:260,h:170,alt:"Graph of [R] concentration against time: a straight line falling from [R0] on the vertical axis, labelled k = \u2013slope.",d:[["l",90,150,90,12,"a"],["l",90,150,255,150,"a"],["t",98,22,"[R\u2080]",""],["l",90,30,215,110,""],["t",165,66,"k = \u2013slope",""],["t",84,76,"[R]","e"],["t",84,90,"concentration","es"],["l",140,162,170,162,"a"],["t",176,166,"time",""]]}},{id:"neet-2026-q088",pn:88,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 3 May",tp:"Chemical Kinetics",st:"Arrhenius equation",q:`Given below is an expression for the rate constant of a first-order reaction occurring at a certain temperature, T (K).
ln k = 14.34 \u2013 (1.25 \xD7 10\u2074)/T
The energy of activation in kcal mol\u207B\xB9 for the reaction is :
(Given: k in s\u207B\xB9, R = 1.987 cal mol\u207B\xB9 K\u207B\xB9)`,o:["12.42","14.34","18.63","24.84"],c:3,s:`Arrhenius equation: k = A e^(\u2013Ea/RT).
ln k = ln A \u2013 Ea/(RT)
Comparing with the given expression, Ea/R = 1.25 \xD7 10\u2074.
Ea = 1.25 \xD7 10\u2074 \xD7 1.987 cal/mol
Ea = 24.84 kcal mol\u207B\xB9`},{id:"neet-2025-q056",pn:56,y:"NEET 2025",tp:"Chemical Kinetics",st:"First order half-life",q:"If the half-life (t\u2081/\u2082) for a first order reaction is 1 minute, then the time required for 99.9% completion of the reaction is closest to :",o:["2 minutes","4 minutes","5 minutes","10 minutes"],c:3,s:`For a 1st order reaction: kt = 2.303 log (A\u2080/A\u209C), where A\u2080 is the initial concentration and A\u209C is the final concentration.
t\u2089\u2089.\u2089% = 10 t\u2081/\u2082.
t\u2089\u2089.\u2089% = 10 \xD7 1 minute = 10 minutes.`},{id:"neet-2025-q089",pn:89,y:"NEET 2025",tp:"Chemical Kinetics",st:"First order rate constant and time",q:`If the rate constant of a reaction is 0.03 s\u207B\xB9, how much time does it take for 7.2 mol L\u207B\xB9 concentration of the reactant to get reduced to 0.9 mol L\u207B\xB9?
(Given: log 2 = 0.301)`,o:["69.3 s","23.1 s","210 s","21.0 s"],c:0,s:`k = 0.03 s\u207B\xB9
t = (2.303)/(k) log (a)/(a \u2212 x)
t = (2.303)/(0.03) log (7.2)/(0.9)
t = (2.303)/(0.03) log 8
t = (2.303)/(0.03) \xD7 3 \xD7 log 2
t = (2.303)/(0.03) \xD7 3 \xD7 0.301
t = 69.3 s`},{id:"neet-2024-q059",pn:59,y:"NEET 2024 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Chemical Kinetics",st:"Arrhenius equation",q:"Activation energy of any chemical reaction can be calculated if one knows the value of",o:["orientation of reactant molecules during collision.","rate constant at two different temperatures.","rate constant at standard temperature.","probability of collision."],c:1,s:`Use log(k\u2082/k\u2081) = (E\u2090/2.303R)(1/T\u2081 \u2212 1/T\u2082).
So E\u2090 can be found if the rate constants k\u2081 and k\u2082 are known at two temperatures T\u2081 and T\u2082.`},{id:"neet-2024-q073",pn:73,y:"NEET 2024 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Chemical Kinetics",st:"Arrhenius equation",q:"Which plot of ln k vs 1/T is consistent with Arrhenius equation ?",o:["(graph 1)","(graph 2)","(graph 3)","(graph 4)"],c:1,s:`Arrhenius equation: k = A e^(\u2013E\u2090/RT).
Taking logs, ln k = ln A \u2013 E\u2090/(RT).
So ln k against 1/T is a straight line with slope = \u2013E\u2090/R and intercept = ln A.
The slope is negative, so the line falls as 1/T increases.`,fig:{w:340,h:310,alt:"Four ln k versus 1/T graphs, numbered 1 to 4",d:[["t",8,45,"(1)","s"],["l",40,130,150,130,"a"],["l",50,130,50,45,"a"],["t",158,138,"1/T","s"],["t",50,36,"ln k","ms"],["l",50,130,135,60,""],["t",173,45,"(2)","s"],["l",215,130,315,130,"a"],["l",215,130,215,45,"a"],["t",323,138,"1/T","s"],["t",215,36,"ln k","ms"],["l",215,55,295,130,""],["t",8,195,"(3)","s"],["l",50,280,150,280,"a"],["l",50,280,50,195,"a"],["t",158,288,"1/T","s"],["t",50,186,"ln k","ms"],["l",52,298,132,226,""],["t",173,195,"(4)","s"],["l",215,280,315,280,"a"],["l",215,280,215,195,"a"],["t",323,288,"1/T","s"],["t",215,186,"ln k","ms"],["l",215,258,300,205,""]]},sfig:{w:290,h:150,alt:"ln k against 1/T is a falling straight line from the intercept ln A on the y-axis with slope minus Ea over R",d:[["l",40,130,240,130,"a"],["l",40,130,40,15,"a"],["t",246,134,"1/T","s"],["t",40,12,"ln k","ms"],["l",40,45,200,115,""],["t",50,30,"Intercept = ln A","s"],["t",130,62,"Slope = \u2013E\u2090/R","s"]]}},{id:"neet-2024-q095",pn:95,y:"NEET 2024 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Chemical Kinetics",st:"Arrhenius equation activation energy",q:`The rate of a reaction quadruples when temperature changes from 27\xBAC to 57\xBAC. Calculate the energy of activation.
Given R = 8.314 J K\u207B\xB9 mol\u207B\xB9, log 4 = 0.6021`,o:["3.80 kJ/mol","3804 kJ/mol","38.04 kJ/mol","380.4 kJ/mol"],c:2,s:`Use log(k\u2082/k\u2081) = (Ea/(2.303R)) \xD7 (1/T\u2081 \u2013 1/T\u2082).
T\u2081 = 300 K, T\u2082 = 330 K, k\u2082/k\u2081 = 4.
log 4 = (Ea/(2.303 \xD7 8.314)) \xD7 (1/300 \u2013 1/330).
Ea = (log 4) \xD7 2.303 \xD7 8.314 \xD7 300 \xD7 330 / 30.
Ea = 3.804 \xD7 10\u2074 J/mol.
Ea = 38.04 kJ/mol.`},{id:"neet-2023-q061",pn:61,y:"NEET 2023 \xB7 7 May",tp:"Chemical Kinetics",st:"Rate law and order",q:"For a certain reaction, the rate = k[A]\xB2[B], when the initial concentration of A is tripled keeping concentration of B constant, the initial rate would",o:["increase by a factor of three","decrease by a factor of nine","increase by a factor of six","increase by a factor of nine"],c:3,s:`Rate r = k[A]\xB2[B].
With [A] tripled, new rate r\u2032 = k(3[A])\xB2[B] = 9k[A]\xB2[B].
So r\u2032 = 9r, an increase by a factor of nine.`},{id:"neet-2023-q072",pn:72,y:"NEET 2023 \xB7 7 May",tp:"Chemical Kinetics",st:"Activation energy",q:`Given below are two statement : one is labelled as Assertion A and the other is labelled as Reason R :
Assertion A : A reaction can have zero activation energy.
Reason R : The minimum extra amount of energy absorbed by reactant molecules so that their energy becomes equal to threshold value, is called activation energy.
In the light of the above statement, choose the correct answer from the options given below :`,o:["A is false but R is true","Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A.","Both A and R are true and R is NOT the correct explanation of A.","A is true but R is false."],c:2,s:`A is true: a few reactions, such as radical reactions, have zero activation energy.
R is true: activation energy is the minimum extra energy reactants absorb to reach the threshold energy.
R only defines activation energy; it does not explain why some reactions have zero of it, so R is not the explanation of A.`},{id:"neet-2022-q051",pn:51,y:"NEET 2022 \xB7 17 Jul",tp:"Chemical Kinetics",st:"Zero and first order graphs",q:`The given graph is a representation of kinetics of a reaction.
The y and x axes for zero and first order reactions, respectively are`,o:["zero order (y = concentration and x = time), first order (y = rate constant and x = concentration)","zero order (y = rate and x = concentration), first order (y = t\xBD and x = concentration)","zero order (y = rate and x = concentration), first order (y = rate and x = t\xBD)","zero order (y = concentration and x = time), first order (y = t\xBD and x = concentration)"],c:1,s:`Zero order: rate r = k[A]\u2070 = k, a constant.
So a plot of rate (y) against concentration (x) is a horizontal line.
First order: t\xBD = 0.693/k, also a constant.
So a plot of t\xBD (y) against concentration (x) is a horizontal line.`,fig:{w:300,h:170,alt:"Graph with y on the vertical axis and x on the horizontal axis; one horizontal straight line parallel to the x axis, with the note Constant temperature T",d:[["l",40,8,40,140,""],["l",40,140,250,140,""],["l",40,56,250,56,""],["t",24,60,"y",""],["t",140,162,"x","m"],["t",76,24,"Constant temperature T",""]]}},{id:"neet-2022-q099",pn:99,y:"NEET 2022 \xB7 17 Jul",tp:"Chemical Kinetics",st:"First order rate constant",q:"For a first order reaction A \u2192 Products, initial concentration of A is 0.1 M, which becomes 0.001 M after 5 minutes. Rate constant for the reaction in min\u207B\xB9 is",o:["0.9212","0.4606","0.2303","1.3818"],c:0,s:`For a first order reaction k = (2.303/t) log([A]\u2080/[A]).
k = (2.303/5) log(0.1/0.001) = (2.303/5) log 10\xB2 = (2.303/5) \xD7 2.
k = 0.9212 min\u207B\xB9.`}]})();
